course: dictionary
lesson: 7851
title: Radioactivity
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Radioactivity is the giving off of invisible energy, called radiation, by the centres (nuclei) of some unstable atoms as they break down. You cannot see it, so scientists use special instruments to detect it.
explain: Context helps you here. «Henri Becquerel discovered radioactivity in 1896; Marie Curie named it, studied it, and won two Nobel Prizes.» The sentence tells you that one scientist found it, and another scientist named it and studied it for her work.
explain: Radioactivity can also be a measure, a number that shows how much radiation something gives off. That is why people talk about «levels of radioactivity». More radiation means higher radioactivity.
fact: Radioactivity is the giving off of invisible energy, called radiation, by unstable atoms.
fact: Radioactivity is also a measure of how much radiation something gives off.
fact: «Radioactivity» is a noun, so it names a thing or idea.
fact: Small amounts of radioactivity are all around us naturally, even in bananas and rocks.
fact: Large amounts of radioactivity are dangerous, so there are safety signs, such as the yellow trefoil.
exercise: What is radioactivity? | A very heavy kind of metal | *The giving off of invisible energy by unstable atoms as they break down | Coloured smoke that comes out of old rocks
exercise: «Marie Curie named it, studied it, and won two Nobel Prizes.» How many Nobel Prizes did she win? | Four | *Two | Three | One
exercise: Why might a scientist write «levels of radioactivity»? | To say radioactivity never changes | To say radiation has no energy | *To show how much radiation something gives off | To describe a kind of staircase
test: «Large amounts of radioactivity are dangerous.» What does this sentence tell us? | Big amounts make things lighter | *Big amounts are harmful, so care is needed | Big amounts cannot be measured | Big amounts are good for plants
test: Who discovered radioactivity in 1896? | Pierre Curie | Marie Curie | *Henri Becquerel | Isaac Newton
exam: Which statement about radioactivity is true? | *Small amounts of radioactivity are found naturally in bananas and rocks | Bananas are made of pure metal | Only nuclear power stations have any radioactivity at all | Radioactivity is always large and must be avoided
